WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.460
I am proposing a universal basic income that
would replace the entire welfare system we

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.549
have now, and one of the first questions that
would come to anyone's mind is, "Can we really

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:22.060
afford this? $10,000 a year for everybody
aged 21 and older until they die?" And the

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:28.230
surprising answer is it would several hundred
million dollars cheaper than the system we

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:35.540
have now. And guess what, by the year 2020,
it will be in the order of $900 billion cheaper.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:42.609
By the year 2025, we're going to be looking
at well into 1.5 to 2 trillion dollars cheaper

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:49.600
than the current system. The right question
is not, "Can we afford universal basic income?"

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.829
The right question is "How are we going to
sustain the current system for the next decade?"

00:00:55.829 --> 00:01:01.769
Something has to be done to deal with an oncoming
fiscal crisis. A universal basic income is

00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:05.790
not only a better system for Americans, it's
also a lot cheaper.

